 "bloques": [
  {
   "tipo": "explicacion",
   "titulo": "Forming the perfect present",
   "texto": [
    "It is formed with **har + participio**: **har reist**, **har sett**, **har bodd**. «Har» also means «have», but in this structure it works as an auxiliary, similar to «have» in «I've traveled.».",
    "It is used to talk about experiences without saying when they occurred, and also about situations that continue: **Jeg har bodd i Norge i to år** («I've been living in Norway for two years.»). If you mention a finished moment, like «Yesterday», you use the simple past."
   ]
  },
  {
   "tipo": "explicacion",
   "titulo": "Common",
   "texto": [
    "Some participles are irregular and need to be learned: **være → vært**, **se → sett**, **dra/reise → reist**, **besøke → besøkt**. In Norwegian, the **har** auxiliary goes before the participle; they are not exchanged."
   ]
  },
